`src/components/discover/ranking.ts:131-165` (`compareOpportunities`) takes a 5th parameter `briefAlignmentOf` implementing the RPT-47 feature — a bet tied to a "standing top strategic bet" sorts higher, one tied to a "challenged assumption" sorts lower. It sits between the Critic-verdict tie-break and the outcome-support tie-break, so a bug here would silently promote or demote real production rankings.

Neither `ranking.test.ts` nor `__tests__/ranking-coverage.test.ts` ever passes a 4th argument to `compareOpportunities`/`rankOpportunities`. The two rationale-text branches it drives (`rationaleFor` lines 199-201: `"on a standing top bet"` / `"a linked bet's assumption is challenged"`) are also never asserted.

```ts
// src/components/discover/ranking.test.ts (add to the tie-break chain describe block)
describe("compareOpportunities — brief alignment tie-break (RPT-47)", () => {
  test("a bet tied to a standing top bet outranks an untied bet at equal ice/verdict", () => {
    const tied = mk({ id: "a" });
    const untied = mk({ id: "b" });
    const briefAlignment = (o: RankableOpportunity) => (o.id === "a" ? 1 : 0);
    expect(compareOpportunities(untied, tied, noCorr, undefined, briefAlignment)).toBeGreaterThan(0);
  });

  test("a bet whose linked assumption is challenged sinks below an untied bet", () => {
    const challenged = mk({ id: "a" });
    const untied = mk({ id: "b" });
    const briefAlignment = (o: RankableOpportunity) => (o.id === "a" ? -1 : 0);
    expect(compareOpportunities(challenged, untied, noCorr, undefined, briefAlignment)).toBeGreaterThan(0);
  });

  test("brief alignment sits below verdict rank: a challenged SHIP still beats an untied WATCH", () => {
    const shipChallenged = mk({ id: "a", critic_review: critic("ship") });
    const watchUntied = mk({ id: "b" });
    const briefAlignment = (o: RankableOpportunity) => (o.id === "a" ? -1 : 0);
    expect(
      compareOpportunities(shipChallenged, watchUntied, noCorr, undefined, briefAlignment),
    ).toBeLessThan(0);
  });

  test("rationale names a standing top bet", () => {
    const opps = [mk({ id: "a" })];
    const ranked = rankOpportunities(opps, noCorr, undefined, () => 1);
    expect(ranked[0].rationale).toContain("on a standing top bet");
    expect(ranked[0].briefAlignment).toBe(1);
  });

  test("rationale names a challenged assumption", () => {
    const opps = [mk({ id: "a" }), mk({ id: "b" })];
    const ranked = rankOpportunities(opps, noCorr, undefined, (o) => (o.id === "b" ? -1 : 0));
    const b = ranked.find((r) => r.opp.id === "b")!;
    expect(b.rationale).toContain("a linked bet's assumption is challenged");
  });
});
```

`src/components/knowledge/graph-visual.ts:118-123` — the doc comment literally says *"Pure function extracted for testability"*, but every test that touches motion state goes through the `usePrefersReducedMotion` hook (`renderHook`), which only covers combinations reachable via `matchMedia` mocking. The pure truth table itself (2 booleans × 2 booleans = 4 cases) has no direct test.

```ts
// src/components/knowledge/__tests__/graph-visual.test.ts
import { computeReducedMotion } from "../graph-visual";

describe("computeReducedMotion (pure truth table)", () => {
  test("false when neither the OS preference nor the toggle asks for reduced motion", () => {
    expect(computeReducedMotion(false, undefined)).toBe(false);
  });
  test("true when the OS media query matches, regardless of the toggle", () => {
    expect(computeReducedMotion(true, undefined)).toBe(true);
    expect(computeReducedMotion(true, "on")).toBe(true);
  });
  test("true when the in-product toggle is 'off', regardless of the OS preference", () => {
    expect(computeReducedMotion(false, "off")).toBe(true);
  });
  test("true when both signals ask for reduced motion", () => {
    expect(computeReducedMotion(true, "off")).toBe(true);
  });
  test("a toggle value other than 'off' does not trigger reduced motion on its own", () => {
    expect(computeReducedMotion(false, "on")).toBe(false);
    expect(computeReducedMotion(false, "")).toBe(false);
  });
});
```

`src/components/cadence/Sketch.tsx:280-289` — when `first.value === 0`, `pct` is `null` and the function falls to the `delta > 0 ? "up to" : "down to"` ternary. Every existing test (`Sketch.test.ts:246`, `Sketch.test.tsx:249`, `sketch-insight.test.ts:32`) only exercises the `[0, 4]` (upward) case; a series like `[0, -4]` — a metric that goes negative from a zero baseline — never hits the `"down to"` string.

```ts
// src/components/cadence/__tests__/sketch-insight.test.ts
it("reads a zero-base series that goes negative as 'down to', not a fabricated percent", () => {
  expect(barInsight(d([0, -4]), fmt)).toContain("Down to -4");
});
```

